---
import Feature from './Feature.astro';
import Shot from './Shot.astro';
import ShotDisclosure from './ShotDisclosure.astro';
---

<section id="features" class="wrap features">
  <Feature eyebrow="Memories by band" title="Your CHIRP memories, organized by band.">
    Import a CHIRP CSV export and your memories become bookmarks with their tones, offsets and
    notes, filed by band. Memories on the same frequency share a row and keep their own details.
    Sort by frequency or name, or tune to the built-in NOAA weather, GMRS, MURS and marine channels.
    <Shot slot="visual" name="sidebar-chirp-import" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Tone discovery" title="Hear a repeater, learn its tone." flip>
    On an NFM channel Leyline shows the CTCSS tone or DCS code it hears beside the bookmark’s tone:
    PL 127.3, or DCS 023. Save it with the bookmark to use when programming your handheld.
    <div slot="visual" class="pair">
      <Shot name="inspector-tone" />
      <Shot name="inspector-dcs" />
    </div>
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Scanning" title="Sweep a band and see what’s on it." stacked>
    Scan a band for carriers, then inspect their frequency, width, signal-to-noise and how often
    they were seen. Results appear under the band and on the band rail. A brief burst reads
    differently from a repeater that is always up. Leyline tells you when the radio is already in
    use.
    <div slot="visual" class="stack">
      <Shot name="scan-2m" frame="terminal" />
      <Shot name="scan-app" />
    </div>
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Decoding" title="Read what the signals carry." stacked>
    Decode ADS-B aircraft, APRS stations, AIS vessels and NOAA weather alerts (SAME). In Decoders,
    follow a message log and a table of transmitters heard, with a map when they report a position.
    Open a message to inspect every field it carried. Name transmitters to recognize them in the app
    and terminal. While a decoder runs, its callsigns appear on the waterfall where they were heard.
    <Fragment slot="details">
      <p>
        Build a watch from a decoder’s fields, such as a callsign, a county, or a place and radius.
        Decoders keep running and watches send macOS notifications with the app closed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Decoding runs on your Mac. Records, labels and watches stay in your Library folder. Maps
        load tiles from Apple.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <div slot="visual" class="stack">
      <Shot
        name="app-decoders-map"
        frame="none"
        caption="Aircraft by transmitted callsign, with the selected message’s fields beside the map. Simulated signals."
      />
      <ShotDisclosure
        label="See the aircraft table"
        name="app-decoders-table"
        caption="Altitude, speed, track and squawk, grouped by callsign. Simulated signals."
      />
      <ShotDisclosure
        label="See decoded callsigns on the waterfall"
        name="app-waterfall-names"
        caption="Decoded APRS callsigns, on the waterfall rows where they were heard. Simulated signals."
      />
    </div>
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Recording and speech" title="Every over, its own clip.">
    Record each transmission with half a second of audio before it starts. The Library keeps the
    clips for playback, and a recording can be run through a compatible decoder later.
    <Fragment slot="details">
      <p>
        Add speech-to-text to search the words, play the matching clip, or export text and SRT.
        Watch a live channel for phrases such as “net control”. Speech sends each over’s audio to
        your OpenAI or OpenAI-compatible provider.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Shot slot="visual" name="library-speech" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="The assistant" title="Ask it to put on the weather radio." stacked>
    Ask the assistant beside the inspector to tune, scan, set the squelch, record or keep a
    bookmark. Open any step to see what it called and what came back. It asks before taking the
    radio from another listener, deleting a recording or starting paid transcription. Stop ends a
    turn. The assistant works from measurements and decoded records, and conversations stay with you
    across restarts.
    <Shot slot="visual" name="app-assistant" frame="none" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Your API key" title="Choose the provider. See what it uses." flip>
    Add an Anthropic, OpenAI or OpenAI-compatible API key in Settings → AI. Choose a model for the
    assistant and a supported provider for speech. Keys are kept in the Mac’s login keychain and
    sent only to the provider or endpoint you chose.
    <Fragment slot="details">
      <p>
        Settings shows your usage and what each feature sends: the conversation and tool results,
        including decoded records, for the assistant; each over’s audio for speech. Listening,
        decoding and recording need no API key.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Shot slot="visual" name="settings-ai" />
  </Feature>
</section>

<style>
  .features {
    padding-top: var(--section-space);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--section-space);
  }

  .stack {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .stack :global(.shot) {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .pair {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 480px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
  }
</style>
